module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.regen_candidate_reverts_hand_authored_mirror

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data regen_candidate_reverts_hand_authored_mirror: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "regen_candidate_reverts_hand_authored_mirror" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "a regeneration installs over a hand-authored file and reports success (THE CANDIDATE TREE CARRIES EVERY MIRROR FILE, INCLUDING THE ONES REGENERATION DOES NOT AUTHOR, so installing the candidate wholesale reverts hand edits to those files while every figure the run prints stays green.",

    "Invalid state: an install step whose population is `every file in the candidate` over a directory that holds BOTH generated and hand-authored files. Harm: hand-authored rows silently disappear -- not only the author's own, but rows other lanes landed earlier, because the candidate's copy is as old as the last build that produced it. The run then reports first_generation_equal and the author reports the verification they genuinely performed BEFORE the install.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable.",

    "RECEIPT, gunbc#10688. Two `NAMESPACE_TRANSITION_ADMISSIONS` rows were authored in `src/v1/stage0/src/namespace_wave_admission.rs`, the phase was run, and it reported both ADMITTED-BY. The regen loop then installed the candidate, which reverted that file to a state predating gunbc#10676 -- deleting the two new rows AND the nine gunbc#10676 rows that had been on main for a day. The commit landed with a message asserting the phase green over bytes that no longer carried the admissions, and `git show --stat` on that commit read 23 insertions / 118 DELETIONS in a file the change was supposed to grow.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVES REVIEW, and it is not inattention: the verification is REAL and it is simply taken at the wrong moment. Build, run the phase, read green, install, commit -- the install sits between the evidence and the artifact, and nothing in the loop's output mentions the file. A second run would have caught it, which is exactly why a loop that ends in an install must not be the last step.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: when an install step copies a produced tree over a source tree, ask which files in the DESTINATION are not authored by the producer. Any such file is reverted on every install, and the revert is silent because the producer never claimed to own it. The tell in the log is the absence of a tell -- the file is not named on either side.",

    "A SECOND RULE, and it is the cheaper one: after any generate-and-install loop, read `git diff --stat` for NEGATIVE line counts on files the change was supposed to grow. A net deletion in a file you added rows to is the signature, and it is visible without knowing anything about the mechanism.",

    "CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed. The generator KNOWS which files it emitted -- it adjudicates them one by one -- so an install whose population is the emitted set rather than the candidate directory cannot touch a file the generator did not author, and no exclusion list is needed. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability: the install step consuming the regeneration's own per-file adjudication roster as its population, so an unadjudicated file in the candidate is not installable rather than being skipped by name.",

    "WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED: nothing in the repository was repaired by this row. The actuator that exhibited it is a local loop script, so the mitigation was an exclusion list of file NAMES -- which is the maintained-roster shape this repository files elsewhere, and it is wrong for the same reason: the next hand-authored mirror file is reverted until someone remembers to add it. The class stands at rung 1.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
